Coastal First Bank is filing this SAR on customer Maria Santos, age 40, from Orlando, Florida, regarding suspected money mule activity and possible movement of business email compromise fraud proceeds through personal checking account 64021875. Santos opened account on September 17, 2025.

At account opening Santos stated she work as medical billing specialist for Central Florida Health Services and reported yearly income around $63,000. She advised account will be for payroll, household bills, personal purchases and normal family expenses. Expected monthly activity was approximately $4,000 to $7,000.

From September 2025 until June 2026 the account activity was generally matching this purpose. Santos received payroll each two weeks for approximately $1,850 to $2,050. Other activity included rent, utilities, groceries, insurance, credit card payments and personal transfers. There was no previous large payments from unrelated companies and no prior outgoing wire transfers.

Starting July 8, 2026 the activity changed significant.

Between July 8 and August 19, 2026 Santos received six ACH credits totaling $165,400 from six businesses not previously seen in account:

July 8: $22,500 from Palmer Architectural Services LLC
July 15: $31,800 from Northfield Medical Equipment Inc.
July 24: $27,400 from Green Valley Property Management LLC
August 2: $19,600 from Horizon Mechanical Group Inc.
August 10: $35,200 from Lakeside Office Technology LLC
August 19: $28,900 from Brighton Engineering Services Inc.

The six payment total was $165,400.

Descriptions on payments included “vendor invoice,” “consulting,” “service payment” and “project.” Coastal First found no ownership, family or employment relation between Santos and these businesses based on available customer records, prior transactions and public record review.

From July 9 to August 20, Santos sent five outgoing domestic wires totaling $151,200:

July 9: $31,000 to Meridian Administrative Solutions LLC
July 17: $28,500 to Harbor Processing Group LLC
July 26: $34,700 to Meridian Administrative Solutions LLC
August 11: $25,000 to Summit Payment Services LLC
August 20: $32,000 to Harbor Processing Group LLC

The five wires total $151,200.

All outgoing wires happened within two business days after one or more of incoming ACH payments. Remaining funds was used for ordinary transactions, some transfers and balance left inside account.

Coastal First did not identify large cash activity, cryptocurrency transfers or international wires during reviewed period.

The transactions was performed through online banking. Login records showed devices previously used by Santos and IP addresses mainly located around Orlando area. Bank did not identify sign of account takeover or unauthorized access.

Public records showed Meridian Administrative Solutions LLC was active Delaware company formed in April 2026. Harbor Processing Group LLC was active Wyoming company formed in May 2026. Summit Payment Services LLC was active Nevada company formed in March 2026. Bank found no ownership connection between Santos and these companies.

On July 28, 2026 Coastal First contacted Santos about commercial ACH credits coming into her personal account.

Santos explained she recently take a part time remote job after answering online employment advertisement. She stated person named “Daniel Morris” told her she will work as payment coordinator for businesses.

According to Santos, Morris told her payments from customers would arrive to her account and she need forward most of money to other company accounts. Santos stated she could keep around 4 percent of some payments as her compensation.

Santos said she communicate with Morris by email and messaging application. She never meet Morris in person.

Bank asked Santos for employment document. Santos provided several emails and screenshots of message but did not provide signed employment contract, employer tax documents, company registration, physical business address or verified telephone contact for Morris.

The messages showed Morris giving Santos amount of expected incoming payments and after payments received, giving instruction for where money should be wired.

On August 13, 2026 Coastal First received fraud inquiry from financial institution that originated the August 10 $35,200 ACH from Lakeside Office Technology LLC.

The originating bank advised their customer reported an employee email account was compromised. Lakeside Office Technology believed they were making payment to normal vendor, but payment instruction was changed and directed money into Santos account.

Coastal First has not independent verified the full allegation and has not received fraud complaints for other five ACH credits.

On August 15, Coastal First contacted Santos again. Santos stated she did not know Lakeside Office Technology and did not know any of other companies sending her payments.

She stated Morris told her before the payments would arrive and she believed this made the job legitimate. Santos said she did not know why businesses could not send funds directly to final recipients.

Santos confirmed she personally authorized outgoing wires because this was her job instruction.

Coastal First reviewed Santos account history again and confirmed this activity was material different than prior use. Before July, account showed payroll from her identified employer and normal personal expenses. There was no history of commercial customer payments, payment-processing activity or outgoing wires.

Coastal First considers activity suspicious because Santos personal account received $165,400 from six unrelated businesses over approximately six weeks, followed shortly by $151,200 in outgoing wires to three business entities with no known relation to Santos.

Santos explained she was receiving and forwarding money at direction of person she met through online employment advertisement, and she was allowed to retain small percentage as payment. She could not provide sufficient records showing legitimate employment or payment-processing business relationship.

The activity is consistent with suspected money mule or third-party payment activity and possible movement of business email compromise proceeds. Fraud inquiry relating to one incoming payment increase the bank concern about source of funds.

Coastal First has not determined that all six incoming payments was fraudulent and has not established Santos knowingly participated in underlying fraud.

Coastal First restricted additional outgoing wire transfers on August 21, 2026 and placed account under enhanced monitoring. Account remains open. Coastal First maintain account statements, ACH records, wire records, online banking and device information, customer communication, due diligence records, public record research and fraud inquiry correspondence and can provide these records to appropriate authorities on request.
